## ASP.NET Framework → ASP.NET Core (.NET 8) Migration

### Pattern Classification

Your code has two **ASP.NET request termination** patterns:

| Call Site | Pattern | Issue in .NET 8 |
|-----------|---------|-----------------|
| `Response.End()` | Thread.Abort to end response | `PlatformNotSupportedException` — API doesn't exist |
| `Response.Redirect(url, true)` | Thread.Abort to skip remaining code | No equivalent; just return from the action |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Swallowing expected abort | Never thrown in .NET 8 — dead code |

---

### Migrated Code (ASP.NET Core MVC)

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;

public class ReportController : Controller
{
    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult Export()
    {
        var data = GenerateReport();

        // Return a FileResult — the framework handles headers and response lifecycle.
        // No Response.Clear/End needed; returning ends the action naturally.
        return File(
            System.Text.Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(data),
            "text/csv",
            "report.csv");
    }

    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult Redirect()
    {
        if (!User.Identity?.IsAuthenticated ?? true)
        {
            // Returning RedirectResult ends execution of this method — no Thread.Abort needed.
            return Redirect("/login");
        }

        try
        {
            ProcessRequest();
            return Redirect("/success");
        }
        // No ThreadAbortException catch — it's never thrown in modern .NET.
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            LogError(ex);
            return Redirect("/error");
        }
    }

    private string GenerateReport() { /* ... */ }
    private void ProcessRequest() { /* ... */ }
    private void LogError(Exception ex) { /* ... */ }
}
```

---

### Key Differences & Pitfalls

| .NET Framework | ASP.NET Core (.NET 8) | Pitfall |
|---|---|---|
| `Response.Write` + `Response.End` | Return `FileResult` / `ContentResult` | Don't write directly to `Response.Body` and then return a result — pick one |
| `Response.Redirect(url, true)` | Return `RedirectResult` | Code **after** a `Redirect()` call still executes if you don't `return` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Remove entirely | Dead code; masks real bugs if left in |
| `Response.Clear()` | Not needed | Framework owns the response pipeline |
| WebForms event handlers (`btnExport_Click`) | Controller action methods with `[HttpPost]` | Route via `[Route]` or conventional routing |

### Critical Pitfall: Code After Redirect Still Runs

In Framework, `Response.Redirect(url, true)` aborted the thread so subsequent code never executed. In Core, **you must `return`** the `IActionResult`:

```csharp
// BUG — code continues executing after Redirect()!
Redirect("/login");
ProcessRequest(); // ← still runs!

// CORRECT — return stops execution
return Redirect("/login");
```

### For Long-Running Requests

If `GenerateReport()` is expensive, honor cancellation:

```csharp
[HttpPost]
public async Task<IActionResult> Export(CancellationToken cancellationToken)
{
    // ASP.NET Core automatically wires HttpContext.RequestAborted
    var data = await GenerateReportAsync(cancellationToken);
    return File(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(data), "text/csv", "report.csv");
}
```

This replaces the old pattern where `Response.End()` would abort work mid-stream — now the token signals cooperative cancellation if the client disconnects.
